  "body": {
    "id": "prop:full-class-nonsharp-length-bracket",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Let \\(\\mathfrak C_n\\) be the collection of all replacement \\((\\epsilon_n,\\delta_n)\\)-differentially private random intervals \\(C\\) satisfying\n\\[\n \\inf_{Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}}\n \\Pr_Q\\{\\tau_Q^{\\mathrm c}(x_0)\\in C\\}\\ge1-\\eta,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\n \\mathcal L_n^*(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}})\n =\\inf_{C\\in\\mathfrak C_n}\\sup_{Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}}\n \\mathbb E_Q[\\operatorname{length}(C)].\n\\]\nIf \\(L_\\pi\\ge\\kappa\\), \\(\\delta_n\\le c_0/n\\), and \\(n\\) is sufficiently large, then\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &c\\left[n^{-\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)}\n \\vee\\{1\\wedge(n\\epsilon_n)^{-\\gamma/(\\gamma+d)}\\}\\right]\\\\\n &\\qquad\\le \\mathcal L_n^*(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}})\\\\\n &\\qquad\\le C\\left[1\\wedge\\left\\{n^{-\\beta/(2\\beta+d)}\n \\vee N_{\\mathrm{priv}}^{-\\beta/(\\beta+d)}\\right\\}\\right].\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe constants depend only on the fixed class constants and \\(\\eta\\).  This is a nonsharp full-class bracket: it does not decide whether the optimal upper exponent is governed by \\(\\gamma\\), by \\(q=\\alpha+\\beta\\), or by a private higher-order Gram term.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "epsilon",
      "delta",
      "Qfull",
      "FullClass",
      "tauCausal",
      "x0",
      "eta",
      "c0",
      "Lpi",
      "kappa",
      "alpha",
      "q",
      "gamma",
      "beta",
      "d",
      "Npriv"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:full-class-ordinary-lower",
      "thm:full-class-direct-regression-upper"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The class \\(\\mathfrak C_n\\) is nonempty because the deterministic interval \\([-1,1]\\) is private and covers every target.  Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}full\\text{-}class\\text{-}ordinary\\text{-}lower}\\) applies to every \\(C\\in\\mathfrak C_n\\); taking the infimum over \\(C\\) preserves its lower bound.  The interval \\(C_n^{\\mathrm{dir}}(h_n)\\) constructed in Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}full\\text{-}class\\text{-}direct\\text{-}regression\\text{-}upper}\\) is one element of \\(\\mathfrak C_n\\), and its uniform mean-length bound gives the upper inequality.  Neither argument invokes the attainable uniform-design class, so both endpoints concern the same class \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}\\).",
    "justification": "The proposition records the strongest currently certified two-sided full-class result rather than leaving the upper endpoint at the trivial length two.",
    "gap": "The lower endpoint uses the smoother contrast, while the direct upper endpoint uses the rougher arm-regression smoothness, so the exact private modulus is not identified.",
    "consumer": "The bracket gives reviewers and privacy-budget committees a valid range for the optimal mean interval length on one unchanged law class.",
